---
title: Basics
order: 1
description: Basic file permissions — chmod, chown, chgrp
tags: [permissions, chmod, chown, chgrp]
---

Unix permissions control read, write, and execute access for three classes: user (owner), group, and others.

@text
Permission structure:

  -rwxrwxrwx
  │└┬┘└┬┘└┬┘
  │ │  │  └── others (o)
  │ │  └───── group  (g)
  │ └──────── user   (u)
  └────────── file type (- = file, d = dir, l = link)

  r = read    (4)
  w = write   (2)
  x = execute (1)

  For directories:
    r = list contents
    w = create/delete files within
    x = access (cd into, traverse)

@text
chmod — change file mode (permissions).

@code bash
# Numeric (octal) mode
chmod 644 file.txt              # rw-r--r--
chmod 755 script.sh             # rwxr-xr-x
chmod 700 private/              # rwx------
chmod 600 secrets.txt           # rw-------
chmod 777 file.txt              # rwxrwxrwx (almost never appropriate)

# Common octal values:
#   755  directories, executable scripts
#   644  regular files
#   600  private files (SSH keys, secrets)
#   700  private directories
#   775  group-writable directories
#   664  group-writable files

# Symbolic mode — more readable, relative changes
chmod u+x script.sh             # add execute for user
chmod g+w file.txt              # add write for group
chmod o-rwx file.txt            # remove all for others
chmod a+r file.txt              # add read for all (a = all)
chmod ug+rw file.txt            # add read+write for user and group

# Set exact permissions with symbolic mode
chmod u=rwx,g=rx,o=r file.txt   # rwxr-xr--

# Remove all permissions for group and others
chmod go= file.txt

# Recursive (apply to directory and all contents)
chmod -R 755 directory/

# Recursive with different permissions for files and directories
find /path -type d -exec chmod 755 {} +
find /path -type f -exec chmod 644 {} +

# Verbose — show changes
chmod -v 644 file.txt

# Only report changes
chmod -c 644 file.txt

@text
chown — change file owner and group.

@code bash
# Change owner
sudo chown alice file.txt

# Change owner and group
sudo chown alice:developers file.txt

# Change group only (note the colon)
sudo chown :developers file.txt

# Recursive
sudo chown -R alice:developers directory/

# Change to match a reference file
sudo chown --reference=ref.txt target.txt

# Don't follow symlinks
sudo chown -h alice link.txt

# Verbose
sudo chown -v alice:developers file.txt

@text
chgrp — change group ownership.

@code bash
# Change group
sudo chgrp developers file.txt

# Recursive
sudo chgrp -R developers directory/

# Change to match a reference file
sudo chgrp --reference=ref.txt target.txt

@text
View permissions and ownership.

@code bash
# List with permissions
ls -l file.txt

# List directory itself (not contents)
ls -ld directory/

# List all files including hidden
ls -la

# Numeric owner/group IDs
ls -ln

# Stat for detailed permissions
stat file.txt
stat -c '%A %a %U %G %n' file.txt    # symbolic, octal, user, group, name
